<title>SWEDEN: Jospin says too early for French view on pact, EMU.</title>
<headline>Jospin says too early for French view on pact, EMU.</headline>
<dateline>MALMO, Sweden 1997-06-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>French Prime Minister Lionel Jospin said on Friday it was too early to give his new government's position on the European Union's economic and monetary union or on the stability pact.</p>
<p>Asked at a briefing in this Swedish city when the position would be announced, he replied in English: &quot;Days or weeks. Things are starting. We don't know when it will be achieved.&quot;  </p>
<p>The EMU stability and growth pact calls for sanctions and fines against countries breaching the EMU deficit limit of three percent of gross domestic product.</p>
<p>Jospin was giving no clues as to his European policy during the 12-hour stopover in Sweden.</p>
<p>&quot;I have just arrived (in office),&quot; said the new 59-year old French leader, who was attending a European Social Democratic Parties' Congress in Malmo, southern Sweden.</p>
<p>&quot;I have begun examining the files.</p>
<p>&quot;On fundamental questions I will not speak here today.&quot;</p>
<p>-- Stockholm newsroom +46-8-700 1004</p>
</text>
